Theorem TF-10: IsShared transfer produces SCALAR sentinel

  Preconditions:
    - Instruction is `IsShared { var }` per Annex E §AIMS TF-10 +
      Appendix A row 11
    - `IsShared` returns the boolean outcome of AIMS's logical sharing
      observation: whether `var` has more than one logical owner credit.
      The boolean is a pure scalar with no ownership-credit consequence.
      A counter, handle flag, tag, side table, or other probe encoding is a
      satisfying physical-plan choice, not part of TF-10.
    - SCALAR is the L-9 sentinel — NOT a lattice element per
      Annex E §AIMS L-9; SCALAR-valued destinations have no
      reference-counting representation

  Soundness property:
    Forall IsShared { var }.
      transfer_tf10(IsShared) = SCALAR
    AND L-6 layer (b) monotonicity holds for the constant function
    f(_) = SCALAR.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part discharge:
      (a) Output verification — TF-10 emits `dst := SCALAR` regardless
          of `var`'s AimsState. Appendix A row 11 shows SCALAR across
          every per-dimension column (Access / Consumption / Cardinality
          / Uniqueness / Locality / Shape / Effect all = SCALAR).
      (b) L-6 layer (b) monotonicity — TF-10 is a constant function:
          for ANY pair of input states (a, b) with a ≤ b,
          f(a) = f(b) = SCALAR; by L-4 reflexivity SCALAR ≤ SCALAR,
          hence f(a) ≤ f(b).

    Part (a) — SCALAR-output verification:
      The IsShared result is a boolean scalar. Per Appendix A row 11
      + Annex E §AIMS L-9, boolean-valued instructions emit the
      SCALAR sentinel — no RC, no lattice-element representation.
      Verified: transfer_tf10() emits Tagged::Scalar regardless of
      input. The boolean is consumed by downstream `Branch` /
      `Select` / `Set` operations which read it as a scalar boolean with
      zero ownership-credit interaction. Its physical encoding is
      projection-owned.
      Note: IsShared is the canonical L-9 sentinel emission site for
      dynamic sharing observations; the SCALAR result is downstream-
      compatible with DP-4 dynamic-COW decision logic.

    Part (b) — constant-function monotonicity:
      Constant f(_) = SCALAR is trivially monotone: forall a, b.
      f(a) = f(b) = SCALAR, and SCALAR ≤ SCALAR holds definitionally
      per L-4 reflexivity (the only valid comparison on SCALAR per
      L-9 — incomparability with non-SCALAR states is intentional
      and not in this proof's scope; non-SCALAR inputs map to SCALAR
      output, which is uniformly comparable to itself only).

    Engines dispatched:
      monotonicity (PRIMARY — L-6 layer (b) per-rule discharge per
        section_04::verify_tf10_isshared_scalar — constant-function
        check)
      case_analysis (PRIMARY — Appendix A row 11 forward-transfer
        enumeration per section_04::verify_tf10_isshared_scalar)
      lattice (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; L-9 SCALAR-not-a-
        lattice-element discharged at §02)
      refinement (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; TF-10 emits SCALAR
        directly, not via refine())

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_04 monotonicity + case_analysis engines discharge TF-10 via SCALAR-output verification + constant-function monotonicity check
